    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sheen is how much light a dried paint film reflects, and nowhere does it matter more than on a ceiling. A ceiling is a large, unbroken surface that light rakes across at a low angle from every window and fixture, so any reflection turns small imperfections into visible lines and bumps. That is why almost every dedicated ceiling paint is flat. The exceptions are rooms where moisture and grease make a slightly tougher, more wipeable film worth the extra reflection.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="why-sheen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Sheen Matters More Overhead
          </h2>

          <p>
            A wall is usually lit head-on, from fixtures and windows that face it. A ceiling is lit from the side: window light enters horizontally and skims across the surface, and fixtures mounted on or near the ceiling throw light along it at a very low angle. That raking light is the harshest possible way to look at a surface, because every bump casts a shadow and every shiny spot catches a highlight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more a paint reflects, the more it shows. A flat paint diffuses light so evenly that drywall seams, nail pops, patches and roller laps all but disappear. An eggshell or satin paint reflects enough that the same flaws show as faint lines and blotches, especially in the afternoon when the sun is low. On a gloss ceiling, every flaw becomes obvious.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheen also affects how even the finish looks, not only how many flaws show. Higher-sheen paints change appearance slightly with film thickness, so the overlaps where two roller passes met, or the band you brushed along the edges, can dry a little shinier or duller than the rest. That is called flashing, and it is much more visible in eggshell and satin than in flat. Our guide to <a href="/ceiling-paint-roller-marks/">ceiling paint roller marks</a> covers it in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is durability. Lower sheens are generally less washable and more likely to mark or burnish if rubbed. On most ceilings that does not matter, since nobody touches them. In bathrooms and kitchens, where moisture and grease accumulate and the ceiling may need cleaning, a little more sheen buys real resistance. The rest of this guide is about where that trade is worth making.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
