        <div id="flat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Flat: The Default for Most Ceilings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flat is the right sheen for the large majority of ceilings: living rooms, bedrooms, dining rooms, hallways, offices and basements. It hides drywall imperfections better than any other finish, it shows roller marks least and it makes touch-ups blend almost invisibly. That combination is exactly what a ceiling needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flat paint's weakness is that it is the least washable. Rubbing it with a damp cloth can burnish it, leaving a shiny patch, and scrubbing can remove paint. On a ceiling that is rarely an issue. The places it can matter are low ceilings in children's rooms or play areas, where hands and toys reach the ceiling, and kitchens directly over a cooktop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most dedicated ceiling paints, including the ones in our <a href="/best-ceiling-paint/">ceiling paint picks</a>, are flat. Some are labelled ultra-flat, which reflects even less light. Ultra-flat is excellent for large ceilings in rooms with big windows or on older plaster ceilings with a lot of surface variation, because it hides the most.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using wall paint on a ceiling, for example because you want it tinted to match the walls, choose its flat version where available. Many wall paint lines offer flat, and that is the closest match to a ceiling paint's appearance. Our comparison of <a href="/ceiling-paint-vs-wall-paint/">ceiling paint and wall paint</a> covers the other differences between the two.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DEFAULT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">When in doubt, go flat.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Unless the room is a bathroom, a kitchen or a space where people will touch the ceiling, flat is the right choice. It hides the most, touches up best and is what nearly every ceiling paint is designed to be.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="matte" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Matte: A Little More Durability
          </h2>

          <p>
            Matte sits just above flat. It reflects slightly more light and is usually a bit more washable, with less tendency to burnish when wiped. Different manufacturers use the word differently, and some matte finishes are very close to flat while others approach eggshell, so it is worth looking at a sample before committing to a whole ceiling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Matte is a good compromise on ceilings that need occasional cleaning but are not in wet or greasy rooms. A low ceiling in a kids' room, a basement family room or a ceiling over a stairwell where hands reach it are good examples. It still hides imperfections well, just not quite as well as flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Matte is also a natural choice when you want a ceiling to match walls that are painted in matte. Using the same paint and sheen on walls and ceiling creates a seamless look with no visible change at the junction, which suits modern rooms with minimal trim. Just roll the ceiling first and keep a wet edge, since matte shows lap marks slightly more than flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            In a bathroom with good ventilation, matte can be enough. Many paint companies sell bath-specific matte finishes with mildew-resistant additives, which give you most of the flaw hiding of flat with better resistance to humidity. In a bathroom without an effective exhaust fan, though, step up to an eggshell or a dedicated mildew-resistant ceiling paint.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Where Matte Fits</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Low</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Ceilings people can reach</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Match</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Matte walls, seamless look</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Vented</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Bathrooms with good fans</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Sample</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Always test first</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="eggshell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Eggshell and Satin: Moisture Over Looks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Eggshell and satin are generally tougher, more moisture-resistant and more washable than flat or matte. On walls those are valuable, and they are the most common sheens for living spaces. On ceilings they are a trade-off: you gain durability and resistance to humidity, and you lose a lot of flaw hiding.
          </p>

          <p>
            The main case for eggshell or satin overhead is a bathroom, laundry room or kitchen where moisture or grease is the bigger problem than appearance. A higher-sheen film sheds moisture better and can be wiped clean of mildew or grease without damaging it. On a small bathroom ceiling with limited raking light, the extra reflection is often barely noticeable.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do use eggshell or satin on a ceiling, preparation matters more. Patch and sand carefully, prime patches so they do not flash, and roll with a consistent, well-loaded roller so every part of the ceiling gets the same film thickness. Any variation in thickness shows as variation in sheen. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-paint-a-ceiling/">how to paint a ceiling</a> covers the method.
          </p>

          <p>
            Semi-gloss and gloss are almost never the right choice for a ceiling. They show every flaw and every roller mark, and they are rarely needed for durability overhead. The exceptions are specialty cases, such as a ceiling in a commercial kitchen or a deliberately glossy decorative finish, and even those usually call for a professional and a sprayer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WATCH FOR</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Higher sheen magnifies prep mistakes.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you choose eggshell or satin for a bathroom ceiling, prime every patch and roll consistently. Unprimed repairs and uneven film thickness both show as dull or shiny patches that flat would have hidden.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sheen by Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Living rooms, bedrooms and dining rooms should almost always get flat. They have large ceilings, often big windows, and nobody touches the ceiling. Flat gives the smoothest look and the easiest touch-ups. If the room has a lot of window light and an older or imperfect ceiling, consider ultra-flat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kitchens are a judgement call. Above the cooking area, grease can settle on the ceiling over time, and a slightly tougher matte or eggshell is easier to clean. If the kitchen has a good range hood that vents outside and the ceiling is large and open to a living area, flat still works well and keeps the ceiling consistent with the rest of the space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bathrooms are where sheen choices matter most. With a strong exhaust fan that runs during and after showers, a mildew-resistant flat or matte ceiling paint works. With weak ventilation, an eggshell or satin bath paint or a dedicated moisture-resistant ceiling paint is safer. In every case, the fan matters more than the sheen; no paint survives a ceiling that stays wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Basements, garages and utility rooms usually get flat for its flaw hiding and low cost. A basement with high humidity may benefit from a mildew-resistant formula. Children's rooms and play areas with low ceilings can use matte for a bit of washability. Hallways and stairwells generally get flat, unless the ceiling is low enough that people regularly touch it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Room</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recommended sheen</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Living room, bedroom, dining</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat or ultra-flat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Best flaw hiding, easy touch-ups</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kitchen</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat or matte</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Matte or eggshell above heavy cooking</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bathroom, good fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat or matte, mildew-resistant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fan matters more than sheen</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bathroom, weak fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Eggshell or satin bath paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moisture resistance first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mildew-resistant if humid</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kids' rooms, low ceilings</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Matte</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some washability</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="texture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Texture, Light and Ceiling Height
          </h2>

          <p>
            Textured ceilings, such as orange peel, knockdown and popcorn, already scatter light and hide small flaws on their own. Sheen matters less there for flaw hiding, but flat is still the usual choice because higher sheens can make texture look blotchy, with the high points catching light. Popcorn ceilings in particular are almost always painted flat, and with care, since the texture can loosen when wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Smooth ceilings are where sheen matters most. A perfectly flat, smooth ceiling looks great in flat paint and can look wavy in anything shinier, because even slight unevenness in the drywall plane catches light. If you are painting a large smooth ceiling, especially one with a long window wall, flat or ultra-flat is the safest choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ceiling height changes how much you see. A high or vaulted ceiling is viewed from further away and usually at a steeper angle, which reduces how visible flaws are. A low ceiling is close to the eye and gets more raking light, so it shows more. That is one reason basements with low ceilings tend to look best in flat, even though some people are tempted to use a satin to brighten them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Natural light matters too. A room with large south- or west-facing windows gets strong, low-angle afternoon light that exaggerates every flaw on the ceiling. A room lit mainly by fixtures is more forgiving. If you are unsure, paint a large sample board in the sheens you are considering and hold it against the ceiling at different times of day.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sample at the ceiling, not the wall.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A sheen that looks fine on a wall sample can look very different overhead in raking light. Hold your sample board flat against the ceiling near the window wall and check it in afternoon light before you buy gallons.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="switching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Switching Sheens on an Existing Ceiling
          </h2>

          <p>
            Going from a higher sheen to flat is straightforward but needs prep. Paint does not grip a glossy surface as well as a dull one, so lightly scuff-sand the existing eggshell, satin or semi-gloss with fine sandpaper and wipe it clean. If the existing paint is oil-based or very glossy, a bonding primer before the flat topcoat is worth the extra step.
          </p>

          <p>
            Going from flat to a higher sheen usually needs two coats to look even, because the absorbent flat surface underneath can make the first coat dry patchy. A coat of primer first helps the sheen come out uniform. Patch and prime any repairs, since higher sheens show them more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Touch-ups are easiest in flat. If you have a ceiling with an existing flat finish and need to fix a small area, the same flat paint will usually blend. With eggshell or satin, small touch-ups often show as shiny or dull spots, and you may need to repaint the whole ceiling from edge to edge to get an even finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are not sure what sheen the current ceiling is, look at it in raking light. Flat reflects almost nothing; matte has a faint glow; eggshell has a soft, noticeable sheen. When in doubt, plan for a light scuff sand, spot priming and two coats, and the result will be even whichever way you go. Our <a href="/best-primer/">primer guide</a> covers which primers bond best to glossy surfaces.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
