        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Eggshell for a Living Room Ceiling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Eggshell is a fine choice for walls, but on a large living room ceiling it reflects window light along every seam and roller lap. Use flat for living spaces and bedrooms. Save eggshell and satin for small, humid ceilings where moisture resistance outweighs appearance.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging Sheen From a Wall Sample</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sheen looks different overhead in raking light than it does on a wall lit head-on. Hold a sample board against the ceiling near the window wall in afternoon light. What looked soft on the wall can look shiny and blotchy on the ceiling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Higher Sheen Without Better Prep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Matte, eggshell and satin show unprimed patches and uneven roller coverage as dull or shiny spots. If you choose a higher sheen, sand patches carefully, prime every repair and keep the roller fully loaded so the film thickness stays even.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Relying on Sheen Instead of Ventilation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A satin bathroom ceiling will still grow mildew and peel if the room stays wet. The exhaust fan is the real fix. Choose a mildew-resistant paint in a suitable sheen, and run the fan during and after showers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Scuff Sand When Going Flat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Flat paint over an old satin or semi-gloss ceiling can peel if the glossy surface is not dulled first. Lightly sand, wipe clean and use a bonding primer over oil-based or very glossy paint before rolling the flat topcoat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sheen for a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flat is the best sheen for most ceilings. It reflects the least light, so it hides drywall seams, patches and roller marks, and it makes touch-ups blend easily. Bathrooms and kitchens are the main exceptions, where a matte or eggshell with mildew resistance may be worth the extra reflection.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is matte or flat better for ceilings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flat hides imperfections slightly better and touches up more easily; matte is a little more washable. For most ceilings flat is the better choice. Matte makes sense on low ceilings people can reach, in well-ventilated bathrooms or when you want the ceiling to match matte walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use eggshell paint on a ceiling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but it shows more flaws. Eggshell resists moisture and cleans better, which is useful in bathrooms and kitchens. On large ceilings with strong window light it will highlight seams and roller laps, so it is best reserved for small, humid rooms with careful prep.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What sheen should a bathroom ceiling be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With a good exhaust fan, a mildew-resistant flat or matte ceiling paint works well. With weak ventilation, an eggshell or satin bath paint is safer because it sheds moisture better. Whatever the sheen, the fan running during and after showers matters more than the paint.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should the ceiling and walls be the same sheen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not necessarily. Many rooms use flat on the ceiling and eggshell or satin on the walls, which is a sensible split. If you want a seamless look with the same color on both, using the same matte paint throughout works well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does flat paint make a ceiling look higher?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Flat paint does not change the actual height, but because it reflects less light and hides flaws, it makes the ceiling recede visually. A lighter color has a bigger effect on perceived height than sheen does. White or a pale tint in flat is the classic choice for making a ceiling feel higher.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is ultra-flat ceiling paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ultra-flat paints reflect even less light than standard flat. They are particularly good for large smooth ceilings with lots of window light and for older plaster ceilings with surface variation, because they hide the most. Several ceiling paint lines offer an ultra-flat finish.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

